  <h1>Hardware Abstraction Layers and the C++ base class</h1>
  <p class="why">Why this matters: lesson 0001 promised that <code class="inline">LlmClient</code>'s design would let you swap Ollama for <code class="inline">llama.cpp</code>. Lesson 0004 said the same about an FPGA backend. This is the lesson where you learn the C++ machinery to actually do it &mdash; the abstract base class &mdash; and the architectural pattern that organises the result, the Hardware Abstraction Layer.</p>
</header>

<h2>1. The concept</h2>

<p>A <b>Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL)</b> is a slice of software that hides "where does the work physically happen" from the rest of the program. The application above the HAL deals in <em>what</em> it wants done; the HAL knows <em>how</em> on this particular hardware. Same idea every time:</p>

<div class="layer-diagram">    +-------------------------------------------+
    |              your application             |
    |        ("ask Wren how she's doing")       |
    +-------------------------------------------+
                          |
                          v  one interface, same call from above
    +-------------------------------------------+
    |          Hardware Abstraction Layer       |
    |   (one abstract base class declares it)   |
    +-------------------------------------------+
              /              |             \
             v               v              v
    +-------------+  +---------------+  +-----------+
    | OllamaHTTP  |  | llama.cpp     |  | XRT FPGA  |
    | over the    |  | linked into   |  | Alveo V80 |
    | localhost   |  | this binary   |  | over PCIe |
    +-------------+  +---------------+  +-----------+</div>

<p>The pattern shows up everywhere:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Arduino's <code class="inline">digitalWrite(pin, HIGH)</code>.</b> Same line of code drives a totally different SoC under each Arduino board.</li>
  <li><b>Linux device drivers.</b> Your program calls <code class="inline">read()</code> on a file descriptor; the kernel routes that to one of hundreds of drivers depending on what's behind it.</li>
  <li><b>SFML's <code class="inline">window.draw(...)</code>.</b> Same call on Windows (DirectX path under SFML) vs Linux (OpenGL under SFML).</li>
  <li><b>Your <code class="inline">LlmClient::processOne()</code>.</b> The rest of the game already doesn't care whether the LLM is HTTP, in-process, or on an FPGA &mdash; <em>but the choice is hardcoded</em>. A HAL formalises it.</li>
</ul>

<p>The <b>shape</b> of every HAL is the same:</p>
<ol>
  <li>One abstract base class declares the operations the application needs ("submit a chat request, return a reply").</li>
  <li>For each piece of real hardware (or transport, or library), a concrete derived class implements those operations.</li>
  <li>The application holds a pointer or reference to the base class. At runtime it calls a method; C++'s <b>virtual dispatch</b> routes to the right derived method.</li>
  <li>The choice of which derived class to use is made at <em>one</em> spot &mdash; usually a factory function or constructor argument &mdash; and nothing above the HAL knows or cares.</li>
</ol>

<h2>2. The C++ machinery &mdash; constructing the base class</h2>

<p>There are exactly four C++ features you need to build a HAL: <code class="inline">virtual</code>, <code class="inline">= 0</code>, <code class="inline">override</code>, and a <code class="inline">virtual</code> destructor. They're small, and they interact in one important way that catches everyone the first time.</p>

<pre class="code"><span class="com">// The abstract base. "= 0" makes it a *pure* virtual function — there is no</span>
<span class="com">// implementation, and the class cannot be instantiated on its own.</span>
<span class="kw">class</span> Hardware {
 <span class="kw">public</span>:
    <span class="com">// (1) virtual destructor — see the callout below; this is the gotcha.</span>
    <span class="kw">virtual</span> ~Hardware() = <span class="kw">default</span>;

    <span class="com">// (2) the interface — pure virtual methods, one per operation.</span>
    <span class="kw">virtual</span> <span class="kw">void</span> turnOn() = <span class="num">0</span>;
    <span class="kw">virtual</span> <span class="kw">void</span> turnOff() = <span class="num">0</span>;
    <span class="kw">virtual</span> <span class="typ">bool</span> isOn() <span class="kw">const</span> = <span class="num">0</span>;
};

<span class="com">// A concrete implementation. "override" tells the compiler "I'm overriding</span>
<span class="com">// a virtual in the base." If the base signature changes, you get a build</span>
<span class="com">// error instead of silently writing a *new* unrelated method.</span>
<span class="kw">class</span> LedOnPinD13 : <span class="kw">public</span> Hardware {
 <span class="kw">public</span>:
    <span class="kw">void</span> turnOn()  <span class="kw">override</span> { digitalWrite(<span class="num">13</span>, HIGH); on_ = <span class="kw">true</span>; }
    <span class="kw">void</span> turnOff() <span class="kw">override</span> { digitalWrite(<span class="num">13</span>, LOW);  on_ = <span class="kw">false</span>; }
    <span class="typ">bool</span> isOn() <span class="kw">const</span> <span class="kw">override</span> { <span class="kw">return</span> on_; }
 <span class="kw">private</span>:
    <span class="typ">bool</span> on_ = <span class="kw">false</span>;
};

<span class="com">// Application code holds a pointer to the BASE. It doesn't know which</span>
<span class="com">// derived class is actually behind it.</span>
<span class="kw">void</span> blink(Hardware&amp; hw) {
    hw.turnOn();
    sleep(<span class="num">1</span>);
    hw.turnOff();
}</pre>

<div class="callout bug">
  <h3>The virtual-destructor gotcha &mdash; the #1 HAL bug</h3>
  <p>If you <code class="inline">delete</code> through a base-class pointer (or use <code class="inline">std::unique_ptr&lt;Hardware&gt;</code>, which does that for you), the destructor that runs must be the <em>derived</em> destructor. That only happens if the base destructor is marked <code class="inline">virtual</code>. Forget it, and you'll silently leak derived-class members &mdash; sockets, file handles, threads &mdash; with <em>no</em> compile-time warning. Make every HAL base class destructor <code class="inline">virtual</code>, every time, without thinking. It is a one-line cost that prevents a whole category of mystery leaks.</p>
</div>

<h3>What each keyword does</h3>

<table class="compare">
  <thead><tr><th>Keyword</th><th>Used on</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code class="inline">virtual</code></td><td>A base-class method</td><td>"Calls to this method through a base-class pointer should look up the derived class's version at runtime." Without it, the call goes to the base version, no matter what's actually behind the pointer.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code class="inline">= 0</code></td><td>A virtual method</td><td>Makes it <b>pure virtual</b>: no implementation, derived classes <em>must</em> provide one. A class with any pure-virtual method is <b>abstract</b> and cannot be instantiated.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code class="inline">override</code></td><td>A derived-class method</td><td>"This is supposed to be overriding a base virtual; please verify." Catches typo'd signatures at compile time. Use it on every override.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code class="inline">virtual</code> destructor</td><td>The base class destructor</td><td>Required if you'll ever <code class="inline">delete</code> a derived through a base pointer. The default is non-virtual, which is dangerous for HAL bases. Always virtual.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>3. In your project &mdash; refactoring <code class="inline">LlmClient</code> into a HAL</h2>

<p>Your <code class="inline">LlmClient</code> is already <em>conceptually</em> a HAL &mdash; the rest of the game doesn't know whether the brain is HTTP, llama.cpp, or an FPGA &mdash; but the choice is currently <b>hardcoded</b> by <code class="inline">httplib::Client</code> being baked into <code class="inline">processOne()</code>. Let's formalise it.</p>

<h3>Step 1 &mdash; pull the interface out into a base class</h3>

<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/LlmBackend.hpp (proposed)</small>
<pre class="code"><span class="pp">#pragma once</span>
<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">"LlmClient.hpp"</span>   <span class="com">// for ChatRequest / ChatReply</span>

<span class="kw">namespace</span> llm_npc {

<span class="com">// HAL base: every backend knows how to turn one request into one reply.</span>
<span class="com">// Everything else (queueing, threading, the public LlmClient surface) stays</span>
<span class="com">// transport-agnostic above this line.</span>
<span class="kw">class</span> LlmBackend {
 <span class="kw">public</span>:
    <span class="kw">virtual</span> ~LlmBackend() = <span class="kw">default</span>;   <span class="com">// REQUIRED — the gotcha from §2</span>

    <span class="com">// One blocking call: the worker thread invokes this. The base doesn't</span>
    <span class="com">// know if it'll be 50 ms or 5 seconds — that's the backend's business.</span>
    <span class="kw">virtual</span> ChatReply request(<span class="kw">const</span> ChatRequest&amp; req) = <span class="num">0</span>;

    <span class="com">// Optional metadata for diagnostics / UI.</span>
    <span class="kw">virtual</span> std::string name() <span class="kw">const</span> = <span class="num">0</span>;

    <span class="com">// HAL safety: most backends shouldn't be copied or moved. Delete them.</span>
    LlmBackend(<span class="kw">const</span> LlmBackend&amp;) = <span class="kw">delete</span>;
    LlmBackend&amp; <span class="kw">operator</span>=(<span class="kw">const</span> LlmBackend&amp;) = <span class="kw">delete</span>;

 <span class="kw">protected</span>:
    LlmBackend() = <span class="kw">default</span>;   <span class="com">// only derived classes call this</span>
};

}  <span class="com">// namespace llm_npc</span></pre>

<h3>Step 2 &mdash; today's HTTP code becomes one derived class</h3>

<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/OllamaBackend.cpp (proposed)</small>
<pre class="code"><span class="kw">class</span> OllamaBackend : <span class="kw">public</span> LlmBackend {
 <span class="kw">public</span>:
    OllamaBackend(std::string host, <span class="typ">int</span> port, std::string model)
        : host_(std::move(host)), port_(port), model_(std::move(model)) {}

    ChatReply request(<span class="kw">const</span> ChatRequest&amp; req) <span class="kw">override</span> {
        <span class="com">// — the EXACT body of the current LlmClient::processOne() —</span>
        httplib::Client cli(host_, port_);
        <span class="com">// ... build JSON, POST /api/chat, parse response ...</span>
    }

    std::string name() <span class="kw">const</span> <span class="kw">override</span> { <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="str">"ollama-http"</span>; }

 <span class="kw">private</span>:
    std::string host_;
    <span class="typ">int</span> port_;
    std::string model_;
};</pre>

<h3>Step 3 &mdash; the FPGA backend from lesson 0004 slots in as another derived class</h3>

<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/XrtFpgaBackend.cpp (proposed)</small>
<pre class="code"><span class="kw">class</span> XrtFpgaBackend : <span class="kw">public</span> LlmBackend {
 <span class="kw">public</span>:
    XrtFpgaBackend(<span class="kw">const</span> std::string&amp; xclbinPath)
        : device_(xrt::device(<span class="num">0</span>)),
          uuid_(device_.load_xclbin(xclbinPath)),
          kernel_(device_, uuid_, <span class="str">"qwen"</span>) {}

    ChatReply request(<span class="kw">const</span> ChatRequest&amp; req) <span class="kw">override</span> {
        <span class="com">// — the hypothetical XRT body from lesson 0004 —</span>
        <span class="kw">auto</span> tokens = tokenize(req);
        inputBuf_.write(tokens.data());
        inputBuf_.sync(XCL_BO_SYNC_BO_TO_DEVICE);
        <span class="kw">auto</span> run = kernel_(inputBuf_, outputBuf_, tokens.size());
        run.wait();
        outputBuf_.sync(XCL_BO_SYNC_BO_FROM_DEVICE);
        ChatReply r; r.id = req.id; r.ok = <span class="kw">true</span>;
        r.content = detokenize(outputBuf_);
        <span class="kw">return</span> r;
    }

    std::string name() <span class="kw">const</span> <span class="kw">override</span> { <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="str">"xrt-alveo-v80"</span>; }

 <span class="kw">private</span>:
    xrt::device device_;   xrt::uuid uuid_;   xrt::kernel kernel_;
    xrt::bo inputBuf_;     xrt::bo outputBuf_;
};</pre>

<h3>Step 4 &mdash; <code class="inline">LlmClient</code> holds a pointer-to-base, not a hardcoded transport</h3>

<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/LlmClient.hpp (proposed edit)</small>
<pre class="code"><span class="kw">class</span> LlmClient {
 <span class="kw">public</span>:
    <span class="com">// Backend is injected at construction; LlmClient owns it via unique_ptr.</span>
    <span class="kw">explicit</span> LlmClient(std::unique_ptr&lt;LlmBackend&gt; backend);
    ~LlmClient();

    <span class="com">// ... submit / drainReplies / etc. all UNCHANGED ...</span>

 <span class="kw">private</span>:
    <span class="kw">void</span> workerLoop();

    std::unique_ptr&lt;LlmBackend&gt; backend_;   <span class="com">// the only thing that changed</span>
    <span class="com">// ... mutexes, CV, queue, atomics, thread — all unchanged ...</span>
};</pre>

<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/LlmClient.cpp (proposed edit)</small>
<pre class="code"><span class="kw">void</span> LlmClient::workerLoop() {
    <span class="kw">while</span> (<span class="kw">true</span>) {
        <span class="com">// ... unchanged: pop a request under the mutex ...</span>

        ChatReply reply = backend_-&gt;request(req);   <span class="com">// VIRTUAL DISPATCH</span>

        <span class="com">// ... unchanged: push reply under the reply mutex ...</span>
    }
}</pre>

<p>One line changed in <code class="inline">workerLoop()</code>. The arrow operator on <code class="inline">backend_</code> looks at the actual derived type at runtime and dispatches to the right <code class="inline">request</code> &mdash; <code class="inline">OllamaBackend::request</code>, <code class="inline">XrtFpgaBackend::request</code>, whatever. The worker, the mutexes, the queue, the atomic counters &mdash; nothing else moves. <code class="inline">main.cpp</code> picks the backend once:</p>

<pre class="code"><span class="kw">auto</span> backend = std::make_unique&lt;OllamaBackend&gt;(
    cfg.host, cfg.port, cfg.model);                 <span class="com">// or XrtFpgaBackend, or...</span>
LlmClient client(std::move(backend));</pre>

<p>Everything else &mdash; <code class="inline">Npc</code>, <code class="inline">Persona</code>, <code class="inline">DialogUI</code> &mdash; is unchanged. They only ever talked to <code class="inline">LlmClient</code>'s public surface, and that surface is unchanged.</p>

<div class="callout real">
  <h3>The payoff lesson 0001 promised</h3>
  <p>Lesson 0001 said you could replace Ollama with <code class="inline">llama.cpp</code> "by changing <code class="inline">processOne()</code>'s body." That was almost-true: today it requires editing <code class="inline">LlmClient.cpp</code>. After this HAL refactor, the swap doesn't need <em>any</em> edit to <code class="inline">LlmClient</code> &mdash; you write a new <code class="inline">LlamaCppBackend</code> alongside <code class="inline">OllamaBackend</code> and pick which one to instantiate. This is the textbook reward for the pattern.</p>
</div>

<h2>4. Often confused with...</h2>

<h3>Abstraction vs. encapsulation</h3>
<p>Both hide things, but for different reasons. <b>Encapsulation</b> is private fields and members &mdash; <em>hiding the internals</em> of <em>one</em> class. <b>Abstraction</b> is hiding which of <em>several</em> implementations is in play behind one interface. <code class="inline">LlmClient</code>'s private mutex is encapsulation; <code class="inline">LlmBackend</code>'s pure-virtual <code class="inline">request</code> is abstraction. You'll mix both in almost every HAL.</p>

<h3>Inheritance vs. composition</h3>
<p>Inheritance ("derived is-a base") is what a HAL needs &mdash; you literally want "Ollama is-a LlmBackend." Composition ("class has-a member") is what you use most other times. The rule of thumb: <em>compose by default; inherit when you genuinely need substitutability</em>. Substitutability is exactly what a HAL is about.</p>

<h3>Virtual dispatch vs. templates (runtime vs. compile-time polymorphism)</h3>
<p>You can get "different code for different backends" two ways. <b>Virtual</b> picks at runtime via the vtable &mdash; tiny indirect call cost (~1 ns), the choice is in a variable. <b>Templates</b> generate a different copy of the calling code per type, picked at compile time &mdash; no runtime cost but no runtime choice either. For a HAL, virtual is almost always the right call: the backend is selected from config at startup, not chosen per-request. Templates win when the choice is fixed at compile time and the call site is in a tight loop.</p>

<h3>Base class vs. interface</h3>
<p>Java and C# have an <code class="inline">interface</code> keyword for "abstract methods only, no data, no implementation." C++ doesn't &mdash; but you can build one by writing a class with <em>only</em> pure virtual methods and a virtual destructor, no data members. That's what <code class="inline">LlmBackend</code> in step 1 above is. Many C++ codebases call them "interfaces" anyway; the language just doesn't have a separate keyword.</p>

<h3><code class="inline">virtual</code> vs. <code class="inline">override</code></h3>
<p>You write <code class="inline">virtual</code> on the <b>base</b> to say "this method dispatches dynamically." You write <code class="inline">override</code> on the <b>derived</b> to say "this method is supposed to override a base virtual; please check." Skipping <code class="inline">override</code> compiles but is risky: if the base later changes signature (e.g. <code class="inline">request(const ChatRequest&amp;)</code> &rarr; <code class="inline">request(ChatRequest&amp;&amp;)</code>), the derived version silently becomes a <em>new unrelated method</em> and the old virtual goes back to the base default at runtime. Use <code class="inline">override</code> on every override.</p>

<h2>5. Quiz</h2>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">You forget <code class="inline">virtual</code> on <code class="inline">LlmBackend</code>'s destructor. <code class="inline">LlmClient</code> owns a <code class="inline">std::unique_ptr&lt;LlmBackend&gt;</code> pointing to an <code class="inline">OllamaBackend</code>. The program runs and exits without crashing. What went wrong?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Compilers don't generally warn about a missing virtual destructor unless they see polymorphic delete in your code — and even then it's a heuristic warning that's easy to miss.">
      Nothing &mdash; the compiler would have refused to compile that code.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. unique_ptr<LlmBackend> calls 'delete' through a base pointer. Without virtual, only the base destructor runs — the derived's fields (host_, port_, model_) are destroyed via the implicit struct teardown, but if they'd been threads/sockets/file handles they would have leaked silently with no warning. The bug is invisible in this trivial case and a memory leak in general.">
      Only the base destructor runs on destruction. Derived-class members get destroyed via the implicit struct teardown, but any derived-class custom cleanup logic is silently skipped.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The derived destructor runs only if the base destructor is virtual. That's the whole point of the rule.">
      The derived destructor runs because <code class="inline">unique_ptr</code> always invokes it directly.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The atomic counter isn't related to destructor virtuality. The bug is about which destructor runs, not about thread safety.">
      The atomic counter <code class="inline">nextId_</code> resets to zero.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">A teammate writes <code class="inline">ChatReply request(ChatRequest req)</code> (by value) on <code class="inline">OllamaBackend</code> instead of <code class="inline">ChatReply request(const ChatRequest&amp; req)</code> as the base declares. They forget the <code class="inline">override</code> keyword. What happens?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="A signature mismatch makes the derived 'request' a new, unrelated method. The base's pure virtual is never overridden, so the class stays abstract and can't be instantiated. Compilation fails, but for a different reason than they expected.">
      Compiles and runs correctly &mdash; C++ allows the by-value variant as a valid override.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Without override, the compiler doesn't emit that warning. That's literally what the override keyword was added in C++11 to enable.">
      The compiler warns about a hidden override but compiles anyway.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. With override removed, the derived method becomes a *new* unrelated method (different signature = different name effectively). The base's pure virtual request() is still not overridden, so OllamaBackend remains abstract — make_unique<OllamaBackend>(...) fails to compile. The override keyword exists precisely to turn this kind of mistake into an immediate, obvious build error rather than a runtime mystery.">
      Compile error: <code class="inline">OllamaBackend</code> is still abstract because the by-value method has a different signature and doesn't count as an override.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Linker errors are the wrong layer. The mistake is caught by the compiler when it sees you trying to construct an abstract class.">
      Linker error at the call site of <code class="inline">backend_-&gt;request()</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">After the HAL refactor, you want to add an <code class="inline">LmStudioBackend</code> for users who prefer LM Studio's HTTP API. Which files do you have to touch?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Persona, Npc, DialogUI live above the HAL — they only talk to LlmClient's public surface. The refactor's whole job is to make this kind of addition cheap.">
      <code class="inline">Persona.hpp</code>, <code class="inline">Npc.cpp</code>, <code class="inline">DialogUI.cpp</code>, and <code class="inline">main.cpp</code>.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Yes. Add LmStudioBackend.hpp/.cpp (a new derived class), and adjust main.cpp (or a factory in main) to construct it instead of OllamaBackend when the config asks for it. Optionally CMakeLists if the new file needs to be compiled. Nothing inside LlmClient changes; nothing above it changes.">
      A new <code class="inline">LmStudioBackend.hpp</code>/<code class="inline">.cpp</code>, the backend-picking code in <code class="inline">main.cpp</code>, and <code class="inline">CMakeLists.txt</code> to compile the new file.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The whole point of the abstraction is *not* to rewrite LlmClient. The refactor put the backend behind a pointer-to-base so you don't have to.">
      <code class="inline">LlmClient.cpp</code> needs a new branch in <code class="inline">workerLoop()</code> to detect the LM Studio case.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Inheritance trees more than one level deep are rarely a clean design choice for a HAL; you'd parallel OllamaBackend, not derive from it. They're different transports, not different kinds of one transport.">
      You inherit <code class="inline">LmStudioBackend</code> from <code class="inline">OllamaBackend</code> because both use HTTP.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">Why does the proposed <code class="inline">LlmBackend</code> base class explicitly delete the copy constructor and copy-assignment operator?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Pure virtual methods alone don't prevent copy semantics — the compiler will still generate copy operations for the data members of derived classes. Disabling copy is a separate, deliberate choice.">
      Pure virtual methods automatically forbid copying.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Backends typically own non-copyable resources — open sockets, FPGA device handles, llama.cpp model state. Default copying would shallow-copy these into broken duplicates. Better to forbid copy at the base, force everyone to pass by pointer/reference, and let unique_ptr<LlmBackend> handle ownership transparently.">
      Backends own non-copyable resources (sockets, device handles, model state); a copy would create a broken duplicate. Force users to pass by pointer/reference instead.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="There's no such thing as 'virtual copy' in C++ in the way virtual dispatch works for methods. The clone idiom exists but is separate; the default copy ctor is non-virtual and that's just how the language works.">
      Otherwise, copies would call the base virtual destructor twice on shutdown.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Copy/move have no special interaction with std::unique_ptr that would cause that bug. unique_ptr itself is non-copyable; that's already a safety net.">
      Without deletion, <code class="inline">std::unique_ptr</code> would silently start copying the backend each time it was passed.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">A junior dev proposes "make <code class="inline">LlmBackend</code> a template instead of a virtual base &mdash; templates are zero-cost." What's the strongest single-sentence argument against?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Modern C++ runs perfectly well with templates; that's not the issue. The issue is when the choice is made, not which feature is more 'modern.'">
      Templates are old C++ style and shouldn't be used in new code.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Templates resolve at compile time — you'd have to pick the backend by recompiling. Virtual dispatch lets you read llm.cfg at runtime, instantiate the matching derived class, and pass it as unique_ptr<LlmBackend>. The vtable indirection costs ~1 ns per call; the request itself costs ~1 second. The 'zero-cost' framing is dominant in the wrong dimension.">
      Templates pick the backend at compile time, but we want to pick it at runtime from <code class="inline">llm.cfg</code> &mdash; and the vtable cost is invisible next to a 1-second HTTP call.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Templates don't lose type information; if anything, they preserve more. The complaint is about *when* the choice is made.">
      Templates lose type information at instantiation, so the wrong backend can be selected.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Template instantiation does add compile time, but that's a secondary practical concern. The architectural answer (compile-time vs runtime choice) is the real one.">
      Templates increase binary size, which matters for an FPGA bitstream.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

